meta {
  name: PersonalConnect
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

post {
  url: {{baseURL}}/google-calendar/personal/connect/
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
}

docs {
  Starts a per-user "connect your own Google Calendar" flow - unlike
  Connect.bru (the one shared organizer account used for appointment sync),
  any signed-in Admin or Associate can use this to connect their own
  personal Google account, so its busy events can be imported into Biopeak
  as availability blocks (see Sync.bru).

  Requests the same scope as Connect.bru (calendar.events - read/write, plus
  userinfo.email). The current sync code (see Sync.bru) only ever reads with
  it - nothing writes to a staff member's personal calendar yet - it's
  granted upfront so a later write-back feature won't need everyone who's
  already connected to reconnect for more scope.

  Returns Google's consent-screen URL as JSON ({"data": {"auth_url": "..."}}).
  The auth_url carries a signed state=<blob> (google_oauth.sign_oauth_state,
  flow="personal", user_id=<your own id, from this request's JWT>) - Google
  echoes it back verbatim to /callback/, which is how that one view knows
  both which flow this is AND which Biopeak user to save the tokens onto,
  since its own request (Google's redirect) carries no JWT of its own.
  Being signed, a tampered state - e.g. someone editing user_id in the URL
  to try to attach their Google account to a different Biopeak login - is
  rejected outright.

  Method: POST
  Path: /google-calendar/personal/connect/
  Source: integrations.google_calendar.views.GoogleCalendarPersonalConnectView
  Auth: Admin or Associate (JWT).

  Manual end-to-end flow (no real frontend running locally):
  1. Run this request. Copy data.auth_url from the response.
  2. Paste auth_url into a real browser, signed in as your own personal
     Google account. Approve consent.
  3. Google redirects to DEV_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI - our own /callback/
     endpoint (see Callback.bru) - which exchanges the code, saves the
     tokens onto your own GoogleCalendarConnection row, and redirects again
     to ADMIN_FRONTEND_URL/#gcal_connected=1 (or #gcal_error=<message> on
     failure, e.g. if you approved with the reserved shared-calendar
     account instead). One browser round trip, nothing to copy-paste back.
  4. Confirm it worked with Status.bru, then run Sync.bru.
}
